5cad3","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q02","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which formula gives the reciprocal of $z\\ne0$?","options":["$z^{-1}=\\overline z$","$z^{-1}=-z$","$z^{-1}=\\dfrac{\\overline z}{|z|^2}$","$z^{-1}=|z|^2\\overline z$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"64b5e17af4b086974043ae6d","time_limit":20},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q03","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For which complex numbers is $z^{-1}$ defined?","options":["For all $z\\in\\mathbb{C}$","For $z\\ne0$","Only for real $z$","Only for $|z|=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"18d7ee6395f5f03d9a937f4f","time_limit":20},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q04","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $(1+2\\mathrm{i})^{-1}$?","options":["$\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}\\mathrm{i}$","$1-2\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{1}{2}-\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6bea316dce36eaffa2896f76","time_limit":20},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q05","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\mathrm{i}^{-1}$?","options":["$\\mathrm{i}$","$1$","$0$","$-\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"97c09d60ce2a8ba37942c90e","time_limit":20},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q06","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the reciprocal of $2$ considered as a complex number?","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$2$","$-2$","$2\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"97cac4bce98a687d56149527","time_limit":20},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q07","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is $|z^{-1}|$ for $z\\ne0$?","options":["$|z|$","$|z|^2$","$1/|z|$","$1/|z|^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"828869001231b86e35cc5d7b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q08","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens to the argument when taking a reciprocal in polar form?","options":["It changes sign, modulo $2\\pi$.","It doubles.","It becomes zero for every $z$.","It becomes the modulus."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1fb7363c757f659b7428bbc6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q09","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes reciprocal from conjugate?","options":["The reciprocal is always exactly the conjugate.","The conjugate is defined only for $z\\ne0$.","The reciprocal changes only the imaginary sign and nothing else.","The reciprocal rescales the conjugate by $1/|z|^2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9d98e8bea8e56a6c4747cc2f","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q10","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $(2-\\mathrm{i})^{-1}$?","options":["$\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\mathrm{i}$","$2+\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{1}{2}+\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1069d075d776e03d9c64abb8","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q11","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $|z|=4$, what is $|z^{-1}|$?","options":["$4$","$2$","$1/4$","$1/16$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f6cb6f626fc1229eee4ab3d8","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q12","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the complex reciprocal of a nonzero $z$ give?","options":["It gives the additive inverse of every complex number.","It gives the reflection across the real axis only.","It gives the multiplicative inverse of a nonzero complex number.","It gives the distance from the origin."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"47f4e70d3b4b7a23d595cc7e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q13","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which mistake about the reciprocal $z^{-1}$ of a complex number should be avoided?","options":["Trying to define $0^{-1}$","Using $z^{-1}=\\overline z/|z|^2$ for $z\\ne0$","Checking that $zz^{-1}=1$","Computing $\\mathrm{i}^{-1}=-\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a1a87b2cfdf7219e1bb063c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q14","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which equation verifies that $w$ is the reciprocal of $z$?","options":["$z+w=0$","$zw=1$","$w=\\overline z$","$|w|=|z|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"61e564ae1e2f18ab2a613118","time_limit":30},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q15","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When is $z^{-1}=\\overline z$?","options":["When $z=0$","When $z$ is real and nonzero only","For every complex number","When $|z|=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"57773e383b17cee74fa773aa","time_limit":40},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q16","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $z=3+4\\mathrm{i}$, what is $z^{-1}$?","options":["$\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{4}{25}\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{3}{25}-\\frac{4}{25}\\mathrm{i}$","$3-4\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"49ff7955010572b0e8495dc5","time_limit":40},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q17","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the condition $z\\ne0$ essential in $z^{-1}=\\overline z/|z|^2$?","options":["Because $\\overline{0}$ is undefined, so the numerator has no meaning.","Because the identity $z\\overline{z}=|z|^2$ fails at $z=0$.","Because $0^{-1}=0$ in $\\mathbb{C}$, so no formula is needed there.","Because $|0|^2=0$, so the formula would divide by zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7ca71f5ee3f5e4bd1e2e1b18","time_limit":40},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q18","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $z=2\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}\\pi/3}$, which polar form represents $z^{-1}$?","options":["$2\\mathrm{e}^{-\\mathrm{i}\\pi/3}$","$\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}\\pi/3}$","$4\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}\\pi/3}$","$\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{-\\mathrm{i}\\pi/3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f36859940208cbe5fa9fc382","time_limit":40},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q19","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair consists of a complex number and its reciprocal?","options":["$1+\\mathrm{i}$ and $1-\\mathrm{i}$","$1+\\mathrm{i}$ and $-1-\\mathrm{i}$","$1+\\mathrm{i}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{i}$","$1+\\mathrm{i}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5630b410865bc6658f3262c9","time_limit":40},{"id":"year-1-2a-math-iii-analysis-complex-reciprocal-html-q20","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement best captures a subtle point about complex reciprocals?","options":["The reciprocal has the same modulus as $z$ and only reverses the sign of its angle.","The reciprocal is always the same as $-z$.","The reciprocal is defined even when $z=0$.","The reciprocal changes both size and angle unless $|z|=1$ or the angle is special."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"95e2789c0c1c7f825440e21c","time_limit":40}]}}